\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N$, $\mathbb Z$, $\mathbb Q$, $\mathbb R$, and $\mathbb C$ denote the natural numbers, integers, rationals, reals, and complex numbers, respectively. All additional parameters and hypotheses are specified in the question.


\paragraph{Statement recorded in the supplied source.}
Is there an infinite finitely presented torsion group, meaning a group in which every element has finite order? The stated Grigorchuk conjecture predicts that no such group exists.
\par\smallskip\textit{Formulation references:} \href{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}{[S1]}, \href{http://aimpl.org/amenablediscrete/5/}{[S2]}.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Is there an infinite finitely presented torsion group, meaning a group in which every element has finite order? The stated Grigorchuk conjecture predicts that no such group exists.

\subsection{Sources}
\begin{itemize}
\item[S1] ULAM.AI and the UnsolvedMath Contributors, UnsolvedMath: A Curated Collection of Open Mathematics Problems, record 20001620 (AIM-GEOMETRIC\_GROUP\_THEORY-0129). Catalogue attribution under CC BY 4.0.
\url{https://huggingface.co/datasets/ulamai/UnsolvedMath}.
